1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,007
That line was one draft away from going out under Harlan's name.

2
00:00:04,007 --> 00:00:08,578
Nobody wrote it to deceive anyone, they were reaching for something that sounded

3
00:00:08,578 --> 00:00:10,463
more concrete than the real spec.

4
00:00:10,463 --> 00:00:14,692
It also had no study behind it, and no amount of small print changes that.

5
00:00:14,692 --> 00:00:19,320
Somewhere in every marketer's instincts is the idea that a small-print disclaimer

6
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:21,434
buys cover for a claim like that one.

7
00:00:21,434 --> 00:00:22,062
It doesn't.

8
00:00:22,062 --> 00:00:26,691
This is the single most common way an ad crosses a line its writer didn't mean to

9
00:00:26,691 --> 00:00:27,033
cross.

10
00:00:27,033 --> 00:00:29,947
A claim is either true and defensible, or it isn't.

11
00:00:29,947 --> 00:00:34,690
Adding "results may vary" underneath a number you invented doesn't move that number

12
00:00:34,690 --> 00:00:36,233
into the defensible column.

13
00:00:36,233 --> 00:00:40,918
It just documents, in writing, that you knew the claim needed a caveat and shipped

14
00:00:40,918 --> 00:00:41,489
it anyway.

15
00:00:41,489 --> 00:00:46,060
A disclaimer does genuinely work around a true but variable outcome, "individual

16
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:49,089
results vary" under an honest before-and-after photo.

17
00:00:49,089 --> 00:00:50,117
That's legitimate.

18
00:00:50,117 --> 00:00:54,803
The mistake is pointing that same small print at a claim that isn't variable, it's

19
00:00:54,803 --> 00:00:57,774
simply false, the way "reduces swelling by 40%" was.

20
00:00:57,774 --> 00:01:01,202
No small print turns an invented number into a measured one.

21
00:01:01,752 --> 00:01:05,987
Advertising law has a name for the actual test running underneath this table:

22
00:01:05,987 --> 00:01:10,331
substantiation, having real evidence behind a claim before you say it out loud.

23
00:01:10,331 --> 00:01:13,631
Could you produce the evidence, right now, if someone asked?

24
00:01:13,631 --> 00:01:17,921
The spec claim passes because it's a manufacturing fact, checkable against the

25
00:01:17,921 --> 00:01:18,745
product itself.

26
00:01:18,745 --> 00:01:23,200
The audience description passes because it's not a claim about an outcome at all.

27
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:27,820
The swelling number fails, disclaimer or not, because the evidence it implies simply

28
00:01:27,820 --> 00:01:28,590
doesn't exist.

29
00:01:28,590 --> 00:01:30,734
That's the whole test, in one question.

30
00:01:30,734 --> 00:01:35,354
Not "does this sound defensible." Could you actually defend it, out loud, to someone

31
00:01:35,354 --> 00:01:36,839
whose job is to ask you to.

32
00:01:36,839 --> 00:01:41,348
Notice the two safe rows pass for different reasons, and that distinction matters.

33
00:01:41,348 --> 00:01:45,858
One survives as a checkable fact, the other because it never claimed an outcome at

34
00:01:45,858 --> 00:01:46,078
all.

35
00:01:46,078 --> 00:01:50,588
Knowing them apart tells you where the real creative freedom lives: you don't lose

36
00:01:50,588 --> 00:01:53,172
specificity or a strong hook by staying honest.

37
00:01:53,172 --> 00:01:57,792
You lose only the shortcut of inventing an outcome instead of describing the product

38
00:01:57,792 --> 00:02:01,367
accurately enough that the description alone does the persuading.

39
00:02:01,917 --> 00:02:05,218
The fix here wasn't softening the claim with a disclaimer.

40
00:02:05,218 --> 00:02:10,000
It was replacing an invented number with a real one Dana could stand behind in front

41
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:11,138
of anyone who asked.

42
00:02:11,138 --> 00:02:15,863
Same product, same confidence in the delivery, a claim that's actually true instead

43
00:02:15,863 --> 00:02:17,627
of one that merely sounds true.

44
00:02:17,627 --> 00:02:20,075
Notice, too, what this rewrite didn't cost.

45
00:02:20,075 --> 00:02:24,629
The delivery is still specific, still concrete, still exactly the kind of detail

46
00:02:24,629 --> 00:02:26,963
Section 2.3 taught you a good hook needs.

47
00:02:26,963 --> 00:02:29,695
Nothing about honesty here required going vague.

48
00:02:29,695 --> 00:02:34,306
It required going true instead of going invented, which turned out to be the same

49
00:02:34,306 --> 00:02:38,859
amount of work as the fabricated version, just aimed at a real fact instead of a

50
00:02:38,859 --> 00:02:39,713
convenient one.

51
00:02:40,263 --> 00:02:44,893
One test, worth running on every claim before it ships, not just the ones that feel

52
00:02:44,893 --> 00:02:45,228
risky.

53
00:02:45,228 --> 00:02:49,691
Say the line out loud, exactly as written, to an imagined compliance officer, or

54
00:02:49,691 --> 00:02:52,870
better, to the actual customer the ad is trying to reach.

55
00:02:52,870 --> 00:02:57,054
If that sentence would survive being asked "how do you know that," ship it.

56
00:02:57,054 --> 00:02:59,564
If it wouldn't, no disclaimer fixes that gap.

57
00:02:59,564 --> 00:03:01,293
Only a different sentence does.

58
00:03:01,293 --> 00:03:03,469
Do this before you generate, not after.

59
00:03:03,469 --> 00:03:08,155
It's far easier to write a defensible line from the start than to catch an overreach

60
00:03:08,155 --> 00:03:12,673
buried in a finished script, especially once it's been performed by an avatar and

61
00:03:12,673 --> 00:03:15,350
sounds convincing coming out of someone's mouth.

62
00:03:15,350 --> 00:03:19,144
A false claim delivered with real conviction is not easier to catch.

63
00:03:19,144 --> 00:03:23,662
It's harder, because the performance itself is doing work to make the words sound

64
00:03:23,662 --> 00:03:24,778
truer than they are.

65
00:03:24,778 --> 00:03:29,185
That's a genuinely new risk this course's own workflow introduces, worth naming

66
00:03:29,185 --> 00:03:31,081
plainly rather than glossing over.

67
00:03:31,081 --> 00:03:33,368
A flat script on a page invites scrutiny.

68
00:03:33,368 --> 00:03:36,157
A confidently performed avatar clip invites trust.

69
00:03:36,157 --> 00:03:40,564
Check the words before the performance gets a chance to earn trust they haven't

70
00:03:40,564 --> 00:03:41,457
actually earned.

71
00:03:42,007 --> 00:03:46,705
Next lecture takes on a different category entirely: not whether a claim is true,

72
00:03:46,705 --> 00:03:51,577
but whether your audience knows they're watching something AI made, and what happens

73
00:03:51,577 --> 00:03:54,767
when a platform decides that disclosure isn't optional.

74
00:03:54,767 --> 00:03:59,639
Run this exact test on your own three video ads from Section 4.6 before you move on.

75
00:03:59,639 --> 00:04:04,279
Say each script's proof line out loud, to yourself, as if you were the one being

76
00:04:04,279 --> 00:04:05,381
asked to defend it.

77
00:04:05,381 --> 00:04:09,789
If any line makes you hesitate even slightly, that hesitation is the signal.

78
00:04:09,789 --> 00:04:14,661
Rewrite it now, while it's one sentence, not later, once it's a finished clip you're

79
00:04:14,661 --> 00:04:15,763
reluctant to touch.

80
00:04:15,763 --> 00:04:18,895
Do the same pass on your statics from Section 3.6 too.

81
00:04:18,895 --> 00:04:23,767
Claims overreach isn't a video-specific risk, and an overlay you generated weeks ago

82
00:04:23,767 --> 00:04:27,943
is just as easy to have forgotten about as a script you wrote yesterday.

83
00:04:27,943 --> 00:04:29,915
The test doesn't change by format.

84
00:04:29,915 --> 00:04:32,235
Only the medium carrying the claim does.
